import json, pathlib
root=pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent
sample_geo={'results':[{'name':'Kampala','latitude':0.3163,'longitude':32.5822,'country':'Uganda','admin1':'Central Region','timezone':'Africa/Kampala'},{'name':'Kampala','latitude':1.2,'longitude':32.1,'country':'Sampleland','timezone':'UTC'}]}
sample_forecast={'daily':{'time':['2026-10-17','2026-10-18','2026-10-19'],'temperature_2m_max':[27.5,28.2,26.8],'temperature_2m_min':[18.3,18.9,19.1],'precipitation_sum':[3.1,0.0,7.5],'wind_speed_10m_max':[12.0,15.0,10.0],'weather_code':[61,2,63]},'timezone':'Africa/Kampala'}
sample_norway={'properties':{'timeseries':[{'time':'2026-10-17T09:00:00Z','data':{'instant':{'details':{'air_temperature':25.0,'wind_speed':3.1}},'next_1_hours':{'details':{'precipitation_amount':0.5}}}}]}}
for name,obj in [('geocoding_sample.json',sample_geo),('open_meteo_sample.json',sample_forecast),('met_norway_sample.json',sample_norway)]: (root/'samples'/name).write_text(json.dumps(obj,indent=2))
def md(s):return {'cell_type':'markdown','metadata':{},'source':s.splitlines(True)}
def py(s):return {'cell_type':'code','metadata':{},'execution_count':None,'outputs':[],'source':s.splitlines(True)}
report_sections=[
('Data flow and program design', '''This project starts with a place name rather than assuming that the user knows latitude and longitude. A place name is convenient but it is not always unique. Open-Meteo's geocoding endpoint searches for matching places and returns a list of candidates. The code deliberately keeps that list instead of immediately accepting the first match. A candidate can be identified by its country, administrative region, coordinates and timezone. In the notebook, `CANDIDATE_INDEX` determines which candidate is used. If the name returns no results, the program reports that clearly rather than indexing into an empty list. This approach makes the example reproducible because the selection is set in the configuration cell, not through a prompt that would interrupt Restart & Run All.\n\nThe main data flow is: **location text → geocoding request → candidate list → selected coordinates → daily forecast request → JSON validation → pandas DataFrame → table, summary and chart**. The forecast request asks for maximum and minimum temperature, total precipitation, maximum wind speed and the weather code. It also requests the location's timezone so that each date represents the local forecast day. The parser checks that all daily arrays exist and have equal lengths. This matters because a table could otherwise mix measurements from different dates. Weather codes are translated into descriptions so that readers do not have to interpret unexplained numbers.\n\nReusable functions and the `Location` data class are kept in `src/weather.py`, rather than copied into notebook cells. The class groups the coordinates, country, administrative area and timezone that belong to a particular place. Functions are used for the HTTP calls, validation and parsing because those operations do not need separate mutable objects. This keeps the design small while still showing appropriate object-oriented modelling.'''),
('Comparing urllib and requests', '''Part A uses two ways of sending the same geocoding request. The standard-library approach combines `urllib.parse.urlencode` with `urllib.request.Request` and `urlopen`, then decodes the response bytes and parses JSON. It needs no additional HTTP dependency. It also gives direct control over the request URL and headers, which is useful when a program must avoid third-party packages. However, there is more boilerplate for reading the response and handling HTTP errors. The `requests` version passes a dictionary through `params=`, which automatically encodes the query string. It makes it straightforward to specify a timeout, inspect the status code, call `raise_for_status()` and parse JSON.\n\nThe notebook reports the status code, Content-Type header and measured response time for a real request when network access is available. A successful status code alone is not enough: a service can return an unexpected payload, so the parser also checks the JSON structure. Response time can change with network congestion and server load. It is therefore an observation from a particular request, not a permanent characteristic of either library. Both approaches are valid; requests is used for the main client because it is easier to combine with retries and error reporting.'''),
('Reliability, retries and failure cases', '''Weather services depend on internet connectivity and third-party infrastructure. A request can fail because the computer is offline, a DNS lookup fails, the server is temporarily unavailable or the response takes too long. The shared request helper applies a timeout to every requests-based call, checks the HTTP status and retries transient failures with exponential backoff. The delay increases after each failed attempt, reducing the chance of repeatedly hitting a struggling server. A retry cap prevents the program from waiting indefinitely. It retries connection and timeout failures and selected transient HTTP codes such as 429 and 5xx. Permanent client errors, such as a typical 404, are reported immediately. After the final unsuccessful attempt, a custom `WeatherAPIError` provides a clear explanation.\n\nValidation also takes place before the network call. Latitude must be between minus 90 and 90 degrees, while longitude must be between minus 180 and 180 degrees. The number of forecast days is restricted to the supported range. The parser checks for missing or unequal daily arrays. Offline tests use saved JSON to confirm that ordinary responses, empty search results, unknown weather codes and malformed forecasts are handled correctly. The notebook demonstrates invalid names, invalid coordinates, a simulated connection failure and a tiny timeout. The tiny timeout may occasionally succeed on a fast connection; the simulated failure gives a repeatable demonstration.\n\nIf live requests fail during a demonstration, the notebook uses clearly labelled saved sample JSON. This is not presented as a current forecast. It lets the table, chart and parsing tests run without an internet connection, but it cannot establish the actual weather on the day the notebook is opened. The source and timestamp of any live observations should always be kept visible.'''),
('Second provider and interpreting differences', '''MET Norway Locationforecast 2.0 provides an independent source that can be used to inspect the same coordinates. Its compact endpoint returns a time series rather than the same daily fields used by Open-Meteo. The project extracts the instant air temperature and wind speed, along with next-hour precipitation when supplied. This is deliberately described as a second-source comparison, not a claim that the two providers publish identical forecasts. For example, Open-Meteo's daily maximum temperature should not be directly compared with one MET Norway instantaneous temperature. Their timestamps, timezones, model choices and aggregation periods differ. A meaningful comparison would first align time periods and units, then calculate daily statistics from the Norwegian series.\n\nMET Norway asks clients to identify themselves through a meaningful User-Agent with contact details. The notebook therefore uses a placeholder in the configuration cell and does not make a live MET Norway call until the student enters their actual university email. No invented contact information is inserted into the shared code. The source is identified whenever its output is displayed. Keeping requests to one selected location and a short forecast period also limits unnecessary API traffic.'''),
('Licensing, usage and a real product', '''The two services are open weather-data providers, but an open endpoint is not automatically an unlimited or unrestricted commercial service. Open-Meteo publishes API documentation and terms distinguishing non-commercial use from commercial offerings, including attribution and access limits. A real product should review the current Open-Meteo terms, check whether its usage qualifies as non-commercial and arrange an appropriate licence or subscription where necessary. The project includes a visible Open-Meteo source statement alongside its forecast output. It does not imply that an academic example grants permission for unlimited commercial traffic.\n\nMET Norway's weather data is published under stated open-data terms and requires proper attribution. Its service also sets usage expectations, including a valid User-Agent, reasonable traffic and respect for caching guidance. Before releasing an application, a developer should check the current terms and the attribution requirements for the exact dataset being used. A commercial service would additionally need monitoring, caching, graceful degradation and a plan for service changes. Terms can change, so the README links to provider documentation rather than treating a brief assignment description as a permanent licence.\n\nThe saved JSON in this repository is a small invented demonstration dataset and is not a historical observation from either provider. It is included only to support offline testing and repeatable visualisation. It should not be used for weather-dependent operational decisions. Any live data used in a published analysis should retain its source, retrieval date and relevant licence notice.'''),
('Privacy, interpretation and limitations', '''Even a simple city search shares information with an external service. When the program sends a location name to the geocoding provider, the provider may also receive technical request metadata such as the originating IP address and time of the request. The forecast provider receives the chosen coordinates, which could be sensitive if they describe a person's home rather than a general city. This assignment uses city-level locations and does not ask for a precise residential address. A real product should explain which providers receive the queries, avoid storing unnecessary search histories and consider privacy notices, retention policies and applicable data-protection rules.\n\nThe charts show a forecast, not a guarantee. Daily precipitation totals can hide the timing of a storm, and maximum wind speed does not tell the whole story about gusts or local exposure. A forecast can change as new observations arrive. The simple summary is useful for comparing days, but it does not replace warnings issued by meteorological authorities. The project also does not include long-term forecast verification, severe-weather alerts, accessibility testing or automated caching. Those would be sensible next steps.\n\nOverall, the exercise demonstrates how a small Python application can move from a user-friendly location name to structured data and readable visualisations while keeping network access, parsing, validation and presentation separate. Its main lesson is that useful API software needs more than a successful HTTP request: it must deal with ambiguity, missing data, outages, provider rules and the privacy implications of the data it sends.''')]
nb=[md('# Mini-Project 1: Fetching and Displaying a Weather Forecast with Python Web APIs\n**MSCS & MSDS OOP with Python — Assignment 2, Advent 2026**\n\nThis notebook uses live Open-Meteo data when available and clearly labelled *invented sample data* when offline. MET Norway is queried only when you provide your actual university email.'),
md('## 1. Configuration\nChange only this cell. Use `CANDIDATE_INDEX` to choose among matching places. Do not publish personal contact details in a public repository.'),
py('CITY = "Kampala"\nCANDIDATE_INDEX = 0\nFORECAST_DAYS = 7\nUNIVERSITY_EMAIL = ""  # Enter your real university email to enable MET Norway\nTIMEOUT_SECONDS = 10\n'),
md('## 2. Imports and project setup'),
py('from pathlib import Path\nimport sys, json\nROOT = Path.cwd().resolve()\nif ROOT.name == "notebooks": ROOT = ROOT.parent\nif str(ROOT) not in sys.path: sys.path.insert(0, str(ROOT))\nimport pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom IPython.display import display\nfrom src.weather import (Location, WeatherError, WeatherAPIError, InvalidLocationError, NoLocationsError, InvalidForecastError, geocode, geocode_requests, geocode_urllib, choose_location, get_forecast, parse_locations, parse_forecast, describe_weather, summary, plot_forecast, get_norway_forecast, parse_norway, request_json, validate_coordinates)\ndef load_sample(filename):\n    with open(ROOT / "samples" / filename, encoding="utf-8") as f: return json.load(f)\nprint("Project folder:", ROOT)\n'),
md('## 3. Part A — HTTP fundamentals\nThe two functions below request the same Open-Meteo geocoding endpoint. The requests version uses `params=` and both use a timeout. The code prints HTTP status, Content-Type and elapsed seconds when connected.'),
py('for method in (geocode_urllib, geocode_requests):\n    try:\n        places, meta = method(CITY, timeout=TIMEOUT_SECONDS)\n        print(method.__name__, "status:", meta["status_code"], "type:", meta["content_type"], "seconds:", meta["response_seconds"], "candidates:", len(places))\n    except WeatherError as exc:\n        print(method.__name__, "unavailable:", str(exc)[:160])\n'),
md('## 4. Part B — Geocoding and daily forecast\nWhen a city name has multiple matches, inspect the candidate table and set `CANDIDATE_INDEX` in the configuration cell. The notebook does not pause for keyboard input.'),
py('LIVE_GEOCODING = True\ntry:\n    candidates = geocode(CITY, timeout=TIMEOUT_SECONDS)\n    if not candidates: raise NoLocationsError("No city matches")\nexcept WeatherError as exc:\n    LIVE_GEOCODING = False\n    print("Live geocoding unavailable; using INVENTED SAMPLE JSON:", exc)\n    candidates = parse_locations(load_sample("geocoding_sample.json"))\nfor i, place in enumerate(candidates): print(f"[{i}] {place.label} ({place.latitude}, {place.longitude}), {place.timezone}")\nlocation = choose_location(candidates, CANDIDATE_INDEX)\nprint("Selected:", location.label)\n'),
py('LIVE_FORECAST = False\ntry:\n    if not LIVE_GEOCODING: raise WeatherAPIError("Offline geocoding: do not mix sample coordinates with live forecast")\n    payload, forecast_meta = get_forecast(location.latitude, location.longitude, FORECAST_DAYS, timezone_name=location.timezone, timeout=TIMEOUT_SECONDS)\n    LIVE_FORECAST = True\nexcept WeatherError as exc:\n    print("Live forecast unavailable; using INVENTED SAMPLE JSON:", exc)\n    payload = load_sample("open_meteo_sample.json")\nforecast_table = parse_forecast(payload)\nprint("LIVE Open-Meteo forecast" if LIVE_FORECAST else "INVENTED SAMPLE — NOT CURRENT WEATHER")\ndisplay(forecast_table)\nprint(summary(forecast_table, location))\n'),
py('figure = plot_forecast(forecast_table)\nplt.show()\n'),
md('## 5. Part C — MET Norway second source\nA real university email is required for the User-Agent. If none is provided, a saved invented sample demonstrates the parser without sending a request. MET Norway values are hourly/instantaneous and must not be treated as directly equivalent to Open-Meteo daily maxima.'),
py('LIVE_NORWAY = False\nif UNIVERSITY_EMAIL.strip() and "@" in UNIVERSITY_EMAIL and not UNIVERSITY_EMAIL.endswith("@example.com") and LIVE_GEOCODING:\n    try:\n        norway_data, norway_meta = get_norway_forecast(location.latitude,location.longitude,contact_email=UNIVERSITY_EMAIL,timeout=TIMEOUT_SECONDS)\n        LIVE_NORWAY = True\n    except WeatherError as exc:\n        print("MET Norway request unavailable:",exc)\nif not LIVE_NORWAY:\n    norway_data = load_sample("met_norway_sample.json")\n    print("INVENTED MET Norway SAMPLE — not live weather. Set UNIVERSITY_EMAIL to enable live request.")\nnorway_table = parse_norway(norway_data)\ndisplay(norway_table.head(8))\nprint("Source:", "MET Norway (api.met.no)" if LIVE_NORWAY else "Invented sample JSON illustrating MET Norway schema")\n'),
md('## 6. Part C — Failure demonstrations\nThe simulated network failure is repeatable. A tiny timeout is attempted only when geocoding was live, because a fast server might still respond.'),
py('import requests\nclass OfflineSession:\n    def get(self,*args,**kwargs): raise requests.ConnectionError("Simulated offline network")\nfor label, action in [\n    ("Invalid city", lambda: geocode(" ")),\n    ("Invalid latitude", lambda: validate_coordinates(1000,32)),\n    ("No matches", lambda: choose_location(parse_locations({"results":[]}))),\n    ("Simulated no network", lambda: request_json("https://example.invalid",session=OfflineSession(),retries=1,backoff=0)),\n]:\n    try: action()\n    except (WeatherError,ValueError) as exc: print(label,"->",type(exc).__name__,str(exc)[:120])\nif LIVE_GEOCODING:\n    try: geocode_requests(CITY,timeout=0.000001)\n    except WeatherError as exc: print("Tiny timeout ->",type(exc).__name__,str(exc)[:120])\nelse: print("Tiny timeout live test skipped because network is unavailable")\n'),
md('## 7. Offline checks (at least five assertions)'),
py('geo = load_sample("geocoding_sample.json")\nweather = load_sample("open_meteo_sample.json")\nassert len(parse_locations(geo)) == 2\nassert parse_locations({}) == []\nassert choose_location(parse_locations(geo),1).country == "Sampleland"\nassert describe_weather(61) == "Slight rain"\nassert describe_weather(999).startswith("Unknown")\nassert len(parse_forecast(weather)) == 3\nassert parse_forecast(weather)["Precipitation (mm)"].sum() == 10.6\nassert len(parse_norway(load_sample("met_norway_sample.json"))) == 1\ntry: parse_forecast({"daily":{"time":["2026-10-17"]}})\nexcept InvalidForecastError: pass\nelse: raise AssertionError("Expected malformed forecast to fail")\nprint("All nine offline assertions passed.")\n'),
md('## 8. Part D — Discussion and limitations (approximately 1,200–1,400 words)')]
for title,body in report_sections:nb.append(md('### '+title+'\n\n'+body.replace('\\n','\n')))
nb.append(md('## References and AI use declaration\n- Open-Meteo API documentation: https://open-meteo.com/en/docs\n- Open-Meteo geocoding documentation: https://open-meteo.com/en/docs/geocoding-api\n- Open-Meteo terms: https://open-meteo.com/en/terms\n- MET Norway Locationforecast documentation: https://api.met.no/weatherapi/locationforecast/2.0/documentation\n- MET Norway API conditions: https://api.met.no/doc/TermsOfService\n- MET Norway licensing information: https://api.met.no/doc/License\n\n**AI declaration (edit to reflect your actual use):** AI assistance was used to draft an initial code structure, tests and explanatory text. I reviewed the code, ran the tests and take responsibility for understanding, adapting and citing the work before submission.'))
notebook={'cells':nb,'metadata':{'kernelspec':{'display_name':'Python 3','language':'python','name':'python3'},'language_info':{'name':'python'}},'nbformat':4,'nbformat_minor':5}
(root/'project1_weather_forecast.ipynb').write_text(json.dumps(notebook,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2))
(root/'tests/test_weather.py').write_text('''import json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport pytest\nfrom src.weather import *\nROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]\ndef sample(name): return json.loads((ROOT/'samples'/name).read_text())\ndef test_locations(): assert len(parse_locations(sample('geocoding_sample.json')))==2\ndef test_missing_results(): assert parse_locations({})==[]\ndef test_ambiguous_selection(): assert choose_location(parse_locations(sample('geocoding_sample.json')),1).country=='Sampleland'\ndef test_weather_code(): assert describe_weather(61)=='Slight rain'\ndef test_unknown_code(): assert 'Unknown' in describe_weather(999)\ndef test_forecast(): assert len(parse_forecast(sample('open_meteo_sample.json')))==3\ndef test_rainfall(): assert parse_forecast(sample('open_meteo_sample.json'))['Precipitation (mm)'].sum()==pytest.approx(10.6)\ndef test_norway(): assert len(parse_norway(sample('met_norway_sample.json')))==1\ndef test_invalid_coordinates():\n    with pytest.raises(InvalidLocationError): validate_coordinates(91,30)\ndef test_no_locations():\n    with pytest.raises(NoLocationsError): choose_location([])\ndef test_missing_forecast():\n    with pytest.raises(InvalidForecastError): parse_forecast({})\ndef test_bad_day_count():\n    with pytest.raises(ValueError): get_forecast(0,32,17)\ndef test_no_network():\n    class Offline:\n        def get(self,*args,**kwargs): raise __import__('requests').ConnectionError('offline')\n    with pytest.raises(WeatherAPIError): request_json('https://example.invalid',session=Offline(),retries=1,backoff=0)\ndef test_retry_transient():\n    import requests\n    class Response:\n        status_code=200; headers={'Content-Type':'application/json'}; url='https://example.invalid'\n        def raise_for_status(self): pass\n        def json(self): return {'results':[]}\n    class Flaky:\n        def __init__(self): self.calls=0\n        def get(self,*args,**kwargs):\n            self.calls+=1\n            if self.calls==1: raise requests.Timeout('temporary')\n            return Response()\n    client=Flaky()\n    data,meta=request_json('https://example.invalid',session=client,retries=1,backoff=0)\n    assert client.calls==2 and meta['status_code']==200\n''')
(root/'README.md').write_text('''# Mini-Project 1 — Fetching and Displaying a Weather Forecast with Python Web APIs\n\nComplete submission package for MSCS/MSDS OOP with Python Assignment 2, Advent 2026.\n\n## Run\n1. Extract the ZIP and open a terminal **inside** `mini_project_1_weather`.\n2. Create a virtual environment if desired: `python -m venv .venv` and activate it.\n3. Install dependencies: `python -m pip install -r requirements.txt`.\n4. Start Jupyter: `jupyter notebook project1_weather_forecast.ipynb` (install `notebook` if needed) or open the notebook in VS Code.\n5. In the first configuration cell set `CITY`, `CANDIDATE_INDEX`, `FORECAST_DAYS` and **your own** `UNIVERSITY_EMAIL`.\n6. Select **Kernel > Restart & Run All**.\n7. Run tests from the project root: `python -m pytest -q`.\n\n**Offline behaviour:** Live calls are attempted first. If unavailable, the notebook uses clearly labelled **invented demonstration JSON**, never presented as current weather. The test suite uses sample JSON and does not need the internet.\n\n## Files\n- `project1_weather_forecast.ipynb`: Part A–D, 1,000–1,500-word report, chart and tests.\n- `src/weather.py`: reusable typed functions, Location class, request retry logic and parsers.\n- `samples/`: invented sample JSON for reproducible offline tests.\n- `tests/test_weather.py`: offline unit tests and simulated connection failure.\n\n## Summary of findings\nGeocoding may return multiple places with the same name. Daily Open-Meteo forecasts can be displayed in a table and temperature/rainfall chart. MET Norway uses different time steps and units, so direct numerical comparison needs care. Reliable API code validates data, limits retries and timeouts, labels sources and avoids publishing personal contact details.\n\n## Provider references and usage\n- Open-Meteo: https://open-meteo.com/en/docs and https://open-meteo.com/en/terms\n- Geocoding: https://open-meteo.com/en/docs/geocoding-api\n- MET Norway: https://api.met.no/weatherapi/locationforecast/2.0/documentation\n- Terms: https://api.met.no/doc/TermsOfService and https://api.met.no/doc/License\n\nCheck current terms and commercial eligibility before using either provider in a real product. Do not commit your actual university email to a public repository; use it only in your local notebook configuration.\n\n## AI use declaration (personalise before submission)\nAI assistance was used to prepare a starting implementation, offline tests and notebook explanations. The student should review, test and adapt all code, and be able to explain each part during the viva.\n''')
print('Created notebook with',len(nb),'cells and report words',sum(len(x.split()) for _,x in report_sections))
